# Releases

[Changesets](https://changesets.dev) computes versions; the pipeline is [`.github/workflows/release.yml`](../../../.github/workflows/release.yml) - read it for the job list. Human-facing "how do I": [`.changeset/README.md`](../../../.changeset/README.md). CI internals: [ci.mdc](ci.mdc); the deploy: [cd.mdc](cd.mdc).

Nothing is published to npm: every workspace is `private: true`, with no `publishConfig` and no `.npmrc`. **A release is a git tag plus a Cloudflare Workers promote.**

## State machine

```text
push to main ──► Release  (concurrency release-main, queue: max, never canceled)
  ├─ gate         ALWAYS. uses ci.yml (full graph, not --affected)
  ├─ select-mode  'version' | 'none'   ('publish' is unreachable here)
  ├─ mode == 'version' → version → open/update the "chore: release" PR   [END]
  └─ mode != 'version' → tag (needs gate) → created? → deploy → uses cd.yml
```

## Invariants

- **`gate` runs on every push to main, in both modes.** While a release PR is open the mode is always `version`, so a gate only on the release path would leave `main` unvalidated for that PR's whole life. Never move it under the `mode != 'version'` branch.
- **The tag is the idempotency key.** `create-release-tag` skips when `vX.Y.Z` exists and reports `created=false`; `deploy` is gated on `created == 'true'`, so re-running `Release` never re-deploys. The existence probe is three-way: 2xx skips, 404 creates, any other failure fails the job rather than guessing. Redeploy on purpose with `cd.yml`'s `workflow_dispatch` and the tag.
- **`mode != 'version'`, not `mode == 'publish'`**: `select-mode` returns `publish` only when publishable packages exist, and all of ours are private, so the equality test would never fire. Load-bearing.
- **`privatePackages.tag: false` is deliberate**: `changeset git-tag` (v3's name for `changeset tag`) would emit one `<app>@X.Y.Z` per app, while the deploy keys on one shared `vX.Y.Z`, which `create-release-tag` cuts instead.
- **`fixed: [["*"]]` is the app set, resting on apps being unscoped**: Changesets matches each `fixed` entry with picomatch against package **names**, and `*` does not cross `/`, so the group is exactly the unscoped workspaces (the apps), never an `@repo/*` package; a new app joins the shared version by existing. Nothing machine-checks the naming underneath (`.syncpackrc.json`'s `@repo/**` group governs dependency specifiers, not workspace names; `pnpm boundaries` never reads a name), so [naming.mdc](../quality/naming.mdc) keeps it by hand: an unscoped package under `packages/` would silently join the group and bump to the app version.
- **`baseBranch: "main"` and `privatePackages.version: true` are load-bearing**: the documented defaults are `"master"` and `{version: false, tag: false}`, which would version nothing here.
- **The version PR branch is force-pushed, not accumulated**: every push to `main` resets `changeset-release/main` from the tip, re-runs `changeset version`, and force-pushes one commit, so it is never behind or in conflict with `main`, and manual edits to it are destroyed.
- **`release.yml` calls `cd.yml` directly** because tags created with `GITHUB_TOKEN` start no workflow ([cd.mdc](cd.mdc)); never "restore" a tag trigger.
- **`queue: max`, not the default**: with `queue: single`, a third push replaces the pending run and that release is silently dropped.

## Recovery

| Fails | Recovery |
|-------|----------|
| `gate` red | Fix on a PR. No tag, no deploy. |
| `version` push rejected | Re-run; the branch is reset from `main` each time. |
| `tag` version drift | Re-align every app in one commit; the action fails closed, naming each app and its version. |
| Tag already exists | `created=false`, deploy skipped. Not an error. |
| `deploy` upload/promote/smoke | The tag stands. "Re-run failed jobs" on the Release run retries the deploy (the `tag` job's `created=true` output is preserved; "re-run all jobs" re-probes, gets `created=false`, and skips - safe, not a retry), or run `cd.yml` via `workflow_dispatch` with the tag. Rollback: `pnpm --filter=<app> exec cf workers deployments create --worker <app>-production --strategy percentage --versions '[{"version_id":"<previous id>","percentage":100}]'`, the previous id from `cf workers deployments list --worker <app>-production`. |

## Repo settings this depends on

- *Actions → General → Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests* enabled.
- The `production` GitHub Environment holding `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` / `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` and the `VITE_API_BASE_URL` variable; CD stays paused until they exist and `CD_ENABLED` is `'true'` ([cd.mdc](cd.mdc)).
- Sentry (optional): the `SENTRY_ORG` variable arms two CD steps per app - `sentry:sourcemaps` between upload and promote, and `sentry:deploy` (set-commits, finalize, deploy marker) after the production smoke. The split is load-bearing: Sentry resolves a `Fixes <SHORT-ID>` issue when set-commits links its commit to a release, so set-commits before promote would resolve issues before the fix is live. Both need the `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` secret and Sentry project slugs equal to the app names; set-commits also needs this repository in the Sentry GitHub integration. Both are `continue-on-error`, so a Sentry outage never blocks a release. `worker-api` also needs its `SENTRY_DSN` Worker secret set once on `worker-api-production` (a `bindings.secret()`), or the upload fails and nothing is promoted.
- Before real traffic: the `production` deployment's `corsOrigins` in `apps/worker-api/cloudflare.config.ts` ships empty, so `/api/*` fails closed with 503 and the CD smoke fails *after* both promotes. Setting it takes a new release - text bindings ship inside the uploaded version, so a CD re-run cannot fix it.
